INT. FERRY SNACK BAR - NIGHT

A late crossing. Fluorescent lights buzz. Vinyl booths, a chrome coffee urn, rain streaking the windows. The deck rolls gently.

LEON PARRISH, 50s, windbreaker, wedding ring worn thin, sits in a booth with a cold sandwich and a newspaper. He has the look of a man avoiding something at home.

A STRANGER, 50s, gray raincoat, hat dripping, slides into the opposite seat without asking. He sets down two cups of tea. Pushes one across.

                    STRANGER
You looked like you needed it.

                    LEON
I didn't ask for tea.

                    STRANGER
No. But you're the type who'd never ask.

Leon eyes the cup. Eyes the man. The ferry horn sounds, long and low.

                    LEON
Do I know you?

                    STRANGER
Not yet.

A WAITRESS drifts past with a pot of coffee. The Stranger nods. She refills nothing. Moves on.

                    LEON
What do you want?

                    STRANGER
Company. It's a long crossing.

                    LEON
Twenty minutes.

                    STRANGER
Long enough.

Leon takes the tea. Sips. It's good. He seems surprised by that.

                    STRANGER (CONT'D)
Where are you headed?

                    LEON
Home.

                    STRANGER
And where's that?

                    LEON
Across the water.

                    STRANGER
(smiling)
That's where everyone's going.

DOLORES KEANE, 60s, in a yellow slicker, appears at the end of the booth with a lunch tray. She sets it down, hard.

                    DOLORES
Leon, I've been looking all over the boat.

                    LEON
Dolores. I told you I'd be back.

                    DOLORES
You said ten minutes. That was an hour ago.

Leon looks at the Stranger, then at her. The Stranger lifts his cup in a small toast.

                    DOLORES (CONT'D)
Who's this?

                    LEON
No one. A man who bought me tea.

                    STRANGER
Gideon Marsh.

He holds out a hand. Dolores doesn't take it. She stares at his face.

                    DOLORES
Gideon Marsh drowned in 1991.

Leon's cup stops halfway to his mouth.

                    GIDEON
Did I?

                    DOLORES
At Pelican Point. Off the ferry slip. I was there.

                    GIDEON
Then you'll remember I wasn't alone.

The fluorescent light flickers once. Twice. Leon puts down his cup and slides to the edge of the booth, his eyes on Dolores.

                    LEON
Dolores. What does he mean?

She doesn't answer. Gideon turns his tea in slow circles.

                    GIDEON
It's all right, Mrs. Parrish. I forgave you a long time ago.

Dolores sits down, very slowly, across from him.

                    LEON
Mrs. Parrish?

The ferry horn sounds again. Land comes closer behind the rain.
